<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: <code>parser</code> fixed, <code>hint</code> won't fix. The
<code>one-day</code> reports are neither, and nothing tracked them until now.
</Callout>

## How a report finds its problem

1. One id, one report. The export's pages overlap, so a repeated row counts once.
2. A report the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) is `one-day`. Nothing in it is
   swapped, so it leaves its tenant's cause.
3. Any other report goes to its cause by `causes.ts`, unchanged: from a tenant on booking-v3 and sent
   through the form, `hint`; from any other tenant on file, `parser`.
4. Intake's tag is not used. It is a guess from the reporter's browser, and the table counts how often
   it disagrees.

## `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

This covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports from <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" of="tenants" />
tenants. Every tenant on file writes the day first, and the shared parser reads `07/04` as 4 July,
so the day and the month trade places: "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July."

Engineering closed this as fixed on 5 October with `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. One
check before anyone relies on it: harbour.dental writes `en-GB`, which puts the day first but is not
an EU locale. If the fix keeps a list of EU locales, it misses them. `causes.ts` asks the platform
instead of keeping a list.

## `hint`: the booking-v3 form prints the month first

This covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports, all sent through the form, from
the <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" of="tenants" /> tenants on booking-v3. The form prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says, and the form reads the day first. The
parser fix does not touch this.

Engineering will not fix it: the booking-v3 form is retired. The tenant directory still lists these
tenants on booking-v3, so the reports stop only when they move off it.

## `one-day`: the date lands one day off

This covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day" /> reports, the ones the desk itself read
as a one-day move. Nothing in them is swapped. "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday", and one meeting landed on
Tuesday for a client in Lisbon and on Monday for a client in Toronto. That points at time zones: a
date with no time of day passed through a clock and came back a day early. The desk footnoted them
under `parser` because of their tenant. Neither the parser fix nor the form touches them, and before
this page they had no ticket.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />

## What #4471 shows

- A subject line says that a date is wrong, not why. Every problem here came in under the same
  subjects, so merging on subject put them in one ticket.
- A footnote is not a ticket. The one-day moves were noticed, then counted under a cause they do not
  have.
- A closed ticket can still hold an open problem. Close the tickets of the problems, not the merge.

